  "preamble": "Lowering of the nucleus of the diphthong [ou]/[ɔu] to [au], parallel to [ei] > [ai]. Middle High German ou (boum, ouge, koufen, gloube) became New High German au (Baum, Auge, kaufen, Glaube), merging with the au that arose from diphthongized ū. In late-20th-century Netherlands Dutch ('Polder Dutch', Stroop 1998) the ou of vrouw and jou was lowered and lengthened to [aːu] alongside ei > [ai]; the change is absent in Flemish.",
